<title>MEXICO: Mexico sees near-balanced fiscal accounts by 2000.</title>
<headline>Mexico sees near-balanced fiscal accounts by 2000.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-06-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Mexican government said on Tuesday the country was aiming to come close to balancing its public sector fiscal accounts by the year 2000.</p>
<p>Outlining its economic plans in its National Program for Financing Development 1997-2000, the government said the public sector balance as a percentage of GDP would be as follows:</p>
<p>    YEAR		AMOUNT	  INCLUDING SOCIAL SECURITY</p>
<p>    1997		 0.2	     (-0.5)</p>
<p>    1998		 0.2	     (-1.3)</p>
<p>    1999		 0.7	     (-0.8)</p>
<p>    2000		 1.2	     (-0.3)</p>
<p>It said the primary balance as a percentage of GDP would be as follows:</p>
<p>    YEAR		AMOUNT	  INCLUDING SOCIAL SECURITY</p>
<p>    1997		 4.1	     (3.4)</p>
<p>    1998		 3.7	     (2.3)</p>
<p>    1999		 4.0	     (2.5)</p>
<p>    2000		 4.3	     (2.8)</p>
<p>((-- Caroline Brothers, Mexico City newsroom +525 728 7905))</p>
